        "content": "Based on the GitHub repository content, here's what **IlluVien** is:\n\n## What It Does\n\nIlluVien is a **drag-and-drop AI agent builder** that allows developers to visually construct LLM (Large Language Model) workflows and deploy them as APIs with a single click. It uses a graph-based editor interface where you can connect different nodes to create complex AI pipelines.\n\n## Core Features\n\n**Modular Building Blocks**\n- Create workflows by dragging and dropping nodes onto a canvas\n- Add custom workflow nodes by creating a single Python file\n- Workflows saved as JSON configs for easy sharing and version control\n\n**Node-Level Debugging**\n- Debug at individual node level rather than debugging entire pipelines\n- Visualize execution flow through the graph\n\n**Performance Evaluation**\n- Built-in evaluation system to test final performance of your workflows\n- Compare different configurations\n\n**Extensibility**\n- Lightweight architecture with minimal dependencies\n- Avoids bloated LLM frameworks\n- Easy to customize and extend\n\n## For Developers\n\nThe tool targets developers who want to:\n- Build AI-powered workflows without extensive coding\n- Visualize and debug LLM pipelines\n- Quickly iterate on agent architectures\n- Deploy workflows as production APIs\n\n**Key benefits highlighted:**\n- \"Easy-to-hack\"\n- JSON-based configuration\n- Supports multiple LLM providers\n- Can work with local models via Ollama\n\n## Technology Stack\n\nThe project appears to use:\n- Python backend\n- Frontend interface\n- PostgreSQL for state storage\n- Docker for deployment\n- Support for various LLM providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, etc.)\n- Local model support via Ollama"
